KG42.register("rules-fdd29b898f26c70daf53e98b",{"id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R001","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R001","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Local-area admission","process_stage":"participation","market_meaning":"Market access should only be granted to community residents or similarly defined groups of market participants. Scope: General microgrid energy market setup recommendations. The information system must provide equal access to every market participant to avoid discrimination. Scope: General microgrid energy market access through the information system. Market participation does not necessarily require participants to be physically connected. Scope: Microgrid energy market participation, including virtual communities. Participation may extend to hyper-local participants. Scope: BMG market setup (C1).","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function admission.local_area under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["admission.local_area"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"BMG market setup (C1). | General microgrid energy market access through the information system. | General microgrid energy market setup recommendations. | Microgrid energy market participation, including virtual communities.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E14","v141-b-g02-e01","v141-b-g02-e02","v141-b-g02-e20"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Market access should only be granted to community residents or similarly defined groups of market participants. Scope: General microgrid energy market setup recommendations.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["admission.local_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g02-e01"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The information system must provide equal access to every market participant to avoid discrimination. Scope: General microgrid energy market access through the information system.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["admission.local_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E14","v141-b-g02-e20"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Market participation does not necessarily require participants to be physically connected. Scope: Microgrid energy market participation, including virtual communities.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["admission.local_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R001-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Participation may extend to hyper-local participants. Scope: BMG market setup (C1).","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["admission.local_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e02"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E03","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p02-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.1 Microgrid energy markets","anchor_text":"ide provide economic beneﬁts [3]. rgy Traditional centralized energy systems can be characterized by","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 2","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Microgrid energy markets provide small-scale prosumers and consumers with a market platform to trade locally generated energy within their community. Hence, they promote the consumption of energy close to its generation and, therefore, foster sustainability and the efficient use of local resources. An exemplary microgrid energy market scenario of residential consumers and prosumers (consumers with photovoltaic (PV) systems) is shown in Fig. 2. Note, that market participants do not necessarily have to be physically connected. Virtual microgrids are the aggregated control of multiple energy producers, prosumers, and consumers in a virtual community."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1 Microgrid setup (C1)","anchor_text":"A clear objective, the deﬁnition of market participants, and the w- form of energy traded must be de","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A clear objective, the definition of market participants, and the form of energy traded must be defined. Microgrids can pursue several, often conflicting objectives [27]. Exemplary objectives are the increase of the security of energy supply or the integration of local renewable generation into the energy supply system. The implementation of the objectives is especially apparent in the design of the pricing method (C4). A microgrid energy market requires a sufficient number of market participants trading energy amongst each other. A subgroup of them needs to have the ability to produce energy. In microgrids this may happen in the form of small prosumers with PV systems [31]. Market access should only be given to the communities’ residents or similar defined groups of market participants. The form of energy traded in the microgrid needs to be defined, e.g. electricity, heat, or a combination thereof. Agents are usually assumed to be self-interested and rational [29]. Additionally, the microgrid setup needs to define whether the traditional energy grid is used for energy transport or a physical microgrid is build."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E14","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Information system (C3)","anchor_text":"p q efﬁcient and reliable information system working in an adequate temporal resolution. It needs to","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A high-performing information system [10] is needed to connect all market participants, provide the market platform, provide market access, and monitor the market operations. This requires an efficient and reliable information system working in an adequate temporal resolution. It needs to be implemented in a way that every market participant has equal access to avoid discrimination. A blockchain protocol based on smart contracts can meet these requirements. Such a blockchain protocol can be seen as a shared global infrastructure for decentralized applications that enables the implementation of full-scale software applications (smart contracts) without a central platform."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1 Microgrid setup (C1)","anchor_text":"A clear objective, the deﬁnition of market participants, and the w- form of energy traded must be de","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A clear objective, the deﬁnition of market participants, and the form of energy traded must be deﬁned. Microgrids can pursue several, often conﬂicting objectives [27]. Exemplary objectives are the increase of the security of energy supply or the integration of local renewable generation into the energy supply system. The implementation of the objectives is especially apparent in the design of the pricing method (C4). A microgrid energy market requires a sufﬁcient number of market participants trading energy amongst each other. A subgroup of them needs to have the ability to produce energy. In microgrids this may happen in the form of small prosumers with PV systems [31]. Market access should only be given to the communities’ residents or similar deﬁned groups of market participants. The form of energy traded in the microgrid needs to be deﬁned, e.g. electricity, heat, or a combination thereof. Agents are usually assumed to be self-interested and rational [29]. Additionally, the microgrid setup needs to define whether the traditional energy grid is used for energy transport or a physical microgrid is build."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e02","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":"y p pp y y g g g y thermore, it empowers the community to take over responsibility for their power s","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Market participants are local consumers and prosumers (C1). Hyper-local participation is also feasible. While only electricity may be traded for now, heat will be included in the future."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Information system (C3)","anchor_text":"p q efﬁcient and reliable information system working in an adequate temporal resolution. It needs to","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A high-performing information system [10] is needed to connect all market participants, provide the market platform, provide market access, and monitor the market operations. This requires an efficient and reliable information system working in an adequate temporal resolution. It needs to be implemented in a way that every market participant has equal access to avoid discrimination. A blockchain protocol based on smart contracts can meet these requirements. Such a blockchain protocol can be seen as a shared global infrastructure for decentralized applications that enables the implementation of full-scale software applications (smart contracts) without a central platform. While data consistency and security are inherent traits of blockchain technology [49], a secure connection from the market participants’ smart meters, which measure and monitor energy generation and demand, to the blockchain is necessary. Secure smart meters can then write the required energy data directly into the corresponding blockchain accounts."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":"No explicit decision variable or variable symbol is defined in the supplied evidence for this admission rule. The source expresses eligibility through procedural categories such as community residents, similar defined groups, virtual communities, and hyper-local participants; introducing an admission predicate or geographic threshold variable would invent unsupported mathematics.","empty_theta_reason":"No parameter, constant, numeric threshold, geographic radius, or resident-share value is specified in the supplied evidence. The source only states qualitative eligibility conditions such as 'communities’ residents or similar defined groups', so theta remains empty rather than inventing a value."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-participation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"admission.local_area","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","quote":"Market access should only be given to the communities’ residents or similar defined groups of market participants."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e01","quote":"Market access should only be given to the communities’ residents or similar deﬁned groups of market participants."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E14","quote":"It needs to be implemented in a way that every market participant has equal access to avoid discrimination."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e20","quote":"It needs to be implemented in a way that every market participant has equal access to avoid discrimination."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E03","quote":"Note, that market participants do not necessarily have to be physically connected."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e02","quote":"Hyper-local participation is also feasible."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"e6132595062dacd5dddbbb0c8bd5e0d047b07851b617eea09b91b3c40c097a02","applicability":"non_mathematical","reason":"The frozen rule is a participation-stage admission policy. Its supplied source statements define eligibility and access requirements — community residents or similarly defined groups, equal information-system access, possible virtual/non-physical participation, and hyper-local participation — but provide no equation, quantitative bound, optimization variable, or parameter defining a local-area threshold. The graph likewise contains only the unary relation admission.local_area with no edges, variables, or parameters. B evidence remains unreviewed; this formalization does not assert independent source verification.","bindings":[],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
